course: euphemisms
lesson: 6
title: Soft words for hard things: the toilet and bodily functions
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Almost every language has soft words for the toilet, because people feel shy about saying what they are going to do there.
explain: Some soft words name a different room or job, like "bathroom" (even with no bath) or "wash my hands" (you will wash them, but that is not why you went).
explain: Others are jokes that make it less awkward, like calling the toilet "the throne" or saying you must "see a man about a dog".
explain: Vivid example: at a fancy dinner a guest says "Excuse me, I must powder my nose" — nobody thinks she only wants make-up; everyone knows she is off to the toilet.
explain: Many of these words belong to one country: Americans ask for the restroom, the British for the loo, and Australians for the dunny.
fact: "Restroom" means a public toilet, mostly in the US; nobody goes there to rest.
fact: "Bathroom" in American English means a toilet, even one with no bath in it.
fact: "Powder room" means a small toilet for guests in a house, mostly in the US.
fact: "The little boys' room" is a joking way to say the men's toilet.
fact: "The little girls' room" is a joking way to say the women's toilet.
fact: "Spend a penny" is an old British way to say use the toilet; public toilets once cost a penny.
fact: "Answer the call of nature" is a joking way to say go to the toilet.
fact: "Freshen up" usually means go to the toilet and wash, often before a meal or meeting.
fact: "I'll just wash my hands" is a polite way to say I am going to use the toilet.
fact: "Powder my nose" is an old-fashioned way, used by women, to say go to the toilet.
fact: "See a man about a dog" is a joking excuse to leave the room, usually to go to the toilet; there is no man and no dog.
fact: "Number one" means a wee, urinating; children and parents use it.
fact: "Number two" means a poo, emptying the bowels; children and parents use it.
fact: "The loo" is the everyday British word for the toilet.
fact: "The john" is American slang for the toilet.
fact: "The facilities" is a polite word for the toilet, as in "Where are the facilities?"
fact: "Comfort station" is an official American word for a public toilet, for example in a park.
fact: "The dunny" is Australian slang for the toilet, often one outdoors.
fact: "The throne" is a joking word for the toilet.
fact: "Break wind" is a polite way to say fart.
exercise: What is a "restroom" in the US? | A public toilet | A room for taking a nap | A staff break room | A quiet room in a library
exercise: An American kid asks "Can I use your bathroom?" What does he want? | To use the toilet | To take a bath | To brush his teeth | To borrow a towel
exercise: What is the "powder room" in an American house? | A small toilet for guests | A room for doing make-up | A store room for flour | A laundry room
exercise: Dad says "I'm off to the little boys' room." Where is he going? | To the men's toilet | To his son's bedroom | To the playroom | To the nursery
exercise: Mum says "Just popping to the little girls' room." Where is she going? | To the women's toilet | To her daughter's bedroom | To a doll's house | To a girls' classroom
exercise: What does "spend a penny" mean in old British talk? | Use the toilet | Buy a cheap sweet | Save money | Pay a small bill
exercise: What does it mean to "answer the call of nature"? | Go to the toilet | Go for a walk in the woods | Answer the phone outside | Listen to birds singing
exercise: At a hotel a guest says "Let me freshen up first." What does she most likely mean? | Use the toilet and wash | Open a window | Buy fresh food | Change the bed sheets
exercise: At a dinner, someone says "Excuse me, I'll just wash my hands." What is the real meaning? | They are going to the toilet | Their hands are dirty from food | They are going to do the dishes | They want to leave the party
exercise: In an old film a lady says "I must powder my nose." What does she mean? | She is going to the toilet | She is putting on make-up | Her nose is cold | She is going to sneeze
exercise: Grandpa says "I need to see a man about a dog." What is he really doing? | Going to the toilet | Buying a puppy | Meeting a dog trainer | Visiting the vet
exercise: A toddler says "I need a number one!" What does she need? | A wee | A poo | The first prize | A drink
exercise: A toddler says "I need a number two!" What does he need? | A poo | A wee | A second helping | His second toy
exercise: In Britain, what is "the loo"? | The toilet | The kitchen | A small garden | A bus stop
exercise: In America, what is "the john"? | The toilet | A man's bedroom | A garden shed | A boss
exercise: A sign says "The facilities are on the second floor." What is on the second floor? | The toilets | The gym and pool | The offices | The lifts
exercise: What is a "comfort station" in an American park? | A public toilet | A first-aid tent | A picnic bench | A bench with cushions
exercise: An Aussie says "The dunny is out the back." What is out the back? | The toilet | The dog | The car | The rubbish bin
exercise: Someone says "Dad's on the throne." Where is Dad? | On the toilet | On a big chair at a party | On the sofa watching TV | In a castle on holiday
exercise: What does "break wind" mean? | Fart | Open a window on a stormy day | Run very fast | Stop a draught
test: A sign at a highway stop reads "Restrooms open 24 hours". What can you do there? | Use the toilet | Sleep for the night | Take a shower and rest | Rent a room
test: Jake texts "brb gotta answer the call of nature lol". What is Jake doing? | Going to the toilet | Going camping | Calling his mum back | Watching a nature show
test: At the wedding, Auntie May says "I'll just go and powder my nose, darling." Where is Auntie May going? | To the toilet | To the make-up artist | Outside for fresh air | To the dance floor
test: "oi where's the dunny mate, I'm bustin" — what does this Australian need? | The toilet | A cold drink | A bus stop | A bandage
test: A teacher's note home: "Toby needs to ask before going to the bathroom." What is Toby asking for? | Permission to go to the toilet | Permission to take a bath | Permission to wash paint off | Permission to see the nurse
test: Gran, in an old story: "Stop the car, I need to spend a penny." What does Gran want? | To use a toilet | To buy something at a shop | To pay for petrol | To give money to a beggar
test: Mid-meeting the boss mutters "Back in five, gotta see a man about a dog." Is anyone getting a dog? | No, he is going to the toilet | Yes, he is buying a puppy | Yes, a dog is coming to the office | No, he is phoning the vet
test: Little Ava on a car trip: "Mummy, is it a number one or a number two?" — "Just a one, sweetie." What does Ava need? | A wee | A poo | To be sick | A snack
test: Someone giggles in class and says "Who broke wind?!" What happened? | Somebody farted | Somebody broke a window | A storm blew the door open | Somebody broke a kite
test: Brother yells "get off the bloody throne, I've been waiting ages!" What does he want? | His turn on the toilet | His seat on the sofa | His gaming chair back | To be king in the game
test: A hotel email says "Feel free to freshen up in the lobby facilities before check-in." What is offered? | Toilets to use and wash in | Free fruit juice | A swimming pool | A place to leave bags
test: A Brit at a party: "Sorry, where's your loo?" What is the guest looking for? | The toilet | The garden | The coats | The kitchen
test: Mia reckons "the john" means the boss in American offices. Is she right? | No, it is slang for the toilet | Yes, it means the boss | Yes, it means a man's office | No, it means the lift
exam: Identify the meaning most accurately conveyed by the expression "to spend a penny" in traditional British usage. | To make use of a toilet | To make a purchase of negligible value | To deposit a coin in a savings account | To donate a small sum to charity
exam: It is given that a notice in a United States park directs visitors to a "comfort station". Ascertain the nature of the facility so designated. | A public lavatory | A rest area furnished with seating | A station providing first aid | A shelter against inclement weather
exam: Select the response consistent with the established meaning of the phrase "to answer the call of nature". | To proceed to a toilet | To respond to a telephone call outdoors | To undertake a walk in the countryside | To observe wildlife in its habitat
exam: Whereas a hostess informs her guests that "the powder room is adjacent to the hall", determine what the guests may find there. | A small toilet for visitors | A room reserved for cosmetics | A storeroom for household powders | A cloakroom for outer garments
